{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["Until I send [it] — an apocopated form [of שָׁל֫וּחַ]: 'that you send your sending' — i.e., that you send the one sent to bring the kid. And it is also customary to use the language of sending [שְׁלִיחוּת] for the dispatch of a gift, as written: ״מִנְחָה הִוא שְׁלוּחָה״ — 'I am sending the kid.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The lemma עד שלחך (Genesis 38:17) has no explicit subject; Rashbam parses שלחך as a defective/shortened form of שָׁל֫וּחַ ('sending'), so the phrase means 'until your sending [arrives]'. The final clause 'I am sending the kid' paraphrases Judah's words from the same verse, not a biblical quotation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:46:38.769648550Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}